<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hadiqat al-Haqiqa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1131</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as The Walled Garden of Truth, Hadiqat al-haqiqa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hadiqat al-Haqiqa, or Walled Garden of Truth, is a long didactic poem by the Persian poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanai">Sana'i</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>, completed around 1131 and dedicated to the Ghaznavid sultan Bahram Shah. Combining ethics, theology and mystical teaching with anecdotes, it was the first great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> epic in Persian and influenced later poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athtar">Attar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hadiqat-al-haqiqa.jpg" alt="Hadiqat al-Haqiqa" width="300" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hadiqat_al_Haqiqa_de_Hakim_Sanai,_Manuscrito_persa_de_1894.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
